{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed. The whole change is in `build_system()` (bot.py:130): the system prompt is now two cache segments instead of one —\n\n1. **Block 1**: `INSTRUCTIONS`, byte-stable, with its own `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}` marker.\n2. **Block 2**: the volatile per-athlete tail (`athlete_context`: date, profile, zones, goal, facts, language), with a second marker.\n\nPreviously a single marker covered both, so every `save_fact` rewrote the tail and — because prompt caching is a prefix hash — evicted the entire ~1000-token block, exactly the pattern in logs/usage.jsonl (`cache_read_input_tokens: 0`, full `cache_creation` right after each memory write). Now a memory write invalidates only the ~200-token tail; the static instructions stay hot. I also updated the README paragraph that described the single-marker caching.\n\n**Verification** (no API key here, so I drove `reply()` with a fake LLM replaying the failure scenario, with data/log files redirected to a temp dir — fixtures untouched):\n- every request has 2 blocks, both cache-marked;\n- the static block is byte-identical across all calls, including after `save_fact`;\n- the tail changes only via the new fact and is byte-identical on a follow-up message (full cache hit in the common case);\n- the tools array stays byte-stable across rounds.\n\nIn production, confirm via the existing `log_usage` output: after a memory write, `cache_read_input_tokens` should cover roughly the instructions and `cache_creation_input_tokens` roughly the tail only.\n\n**ANSWER.md** is written, including which parts of the colleague's note applied: the two-segment fix and the verification-via-usage-fields applied directly; the refusal of top-N fact selection applied by not building it (all facts stay in the tail, keeping it byte-stable); keyword tool filtering was already in place per NOTES.md and left alone; the \"rarest-changing first\" tail ordering was noted as cosmetic and skipped."}
